    visualizations: [
      {
        title: 'DORA four keys + Elite/High/Medium/Low bands',
        description: 'Deployment Frequency × Lead Time for Changes × Change Failure Rate × MTTR. Elite teams: multiple deploys/day, < 1 day lead time, < 15% failure, < 1 hour MTTR.',
        image: '/diagrams/devops/a2-dora-metrics.png',
      },
    ],
    introduction: `DORA (DevOps Research and Assessment) is a research program founded by Nicole Forsgren, Jez Humble, and Gene Kim in ~2014, acquired by Google in 2018 and now run as part of Google Cloud. The annual State of DevOps Report has 9+ years of survey data from tens of thousands of respondents across every industry.

The DORA framework names four metrics that statistically distinguish high-performing software teams. Verbatim from the Accelerate book and dora.dev:

1. Deployment Frequency — how often the organization successfully releases to production. Counter, not duration. Range: multiple per day → less than once per six months.

2. Lead Time for Changes — time from code committed to code running in production. Includes review, build, test, deploy time. Range: less than one hour → more than six months.

3. Change Failure Rate — percentage of deployments that cause a failure in production (incident, rollback, hotfix required). Range: 0-15% (elite) → 46-60% (low).

4. Mean Time to Restore (MTTR) — time to restore service when an incident occurs. Range: less than one hour (elite) → more than six months (low).

The first two measure throughput (velocity). The last two measure stability. Accelerate\'s key finding: high-performing teams excel at BOTH simultaneously. The "speed vs stability" trade-off is a myth at the team level — teams that ship more often have FEWER incidents, not more.

Performance bands (2024 State of DevOps Report, classic four-band model):

Elite performers:
- Deployment Frequency: on-demand, multiple deploys per day
- Lead Time: less than one day
- Change Failure Rate: 0-15%
- MTTR: less than one hour

High performers:
- Deployment Frequency: between once per day and once per week
- Lead Time: between one day and one week
- Change Failure Rate: 16-30%
- MTTR: less than one day

Medium performers:
- Deployment Frequency: between once per week and once per month
- Lead Time: between one week and one month
- Change Failure Rate: 16-30%
- MTTR: between one day and one week

Low performers:
- Deployment Frequency: between once per month and once every six months
- Lead Time: between one month and six months
- Change Failure Rate: 46-60%
- MTTR: between one week and one month

(The 2025 report introduces an Archetype framework that replaces the four bands with cluster analysis. The classic bands remain useful for maturity assessment.)

The fifth metric (added in 2021, refined since): Reliability — does the service meet the customer\'s SLOs? The Accelerate research team added this after recognizing that velocity + stability still leaves a gap when measured services don\'t actually meet user expectations.

Why this matters in interviews: DORA metrics are the LINGUA FRANCA of senior DevOps conversations. A senior engineer in 2026 should be able to:
- Recite the four keys without notes
- Quote band thresholds (elite vs high vs medium vs low)
- Explain why Accelerate found speed and stability are NOT trade-offs
- Apply DORA to diagnose a team\'s performance bottleneck`,
