    description: 'Skelton & Pais (2019) framework for organizing teams to maximize fast flow. Four team types + three interaction modes.',
    visualizations: [
      {
        title: 'Team Topologies — 4 team types + 3 interaction modes',
        description: 'Stream-aligned (build the product) + Enabling (consult) + Platform (self-service) + Complicated Subsystem (specialist). Interactions: collaboration, X-as-a-Service, facilitating.',
        image: '/diagrams/devops/a4-team-topologies.png',
      },
    ],
    introduction: `Team Topologies (Matthew Skelton & Manuel Pais, 2019; 2nd ed. updates ongoing) is the canonical organizational design framework for DevOps-aligned teams. It builds on Conway\'s Law and the cognitive-load research of Eduardo Salas et al. The book is the most-cited org-design text in modern DevOps.

The thesis: organize teams to MINIMIZE COGNITIVE LOAD and MAXIMIZE FAST FLOW. Four team types, each with a specific purpose, interacting via three defined modes.

THE FOUR TEAM TYPES:

1. Stream-Aligned Team (the default; ~80% of teams)
- Aligned to a single, valuable stream of work — typically a product, customer journey, or business capability.
- End-to-end ownership: design, build, test, deploy, operate, retire.
- Empowered to deliver value without waiting on other teams.
- Typical size: 5-9 people (two-pizza team rule).
- Examples: "Checkout team," "Payments team," "Search team."

2. Enabling Team (specialists who teach)
- Help stream-aligned teams overcome obstacles or develop missing capabilities.
- Time-boxed engagements: come in, transfer knowledge, leave.
- Examples: "Performance Enabling team" helps a stream-aligned team optimize a slow service; "Security Enabling team" helps adopt threat modeling.
- NOT permanent operators of the capability — they teach, then withdraw.

3. Complicated Subsystem Team (specialists who own)
- Build and maintain a subsystem requiring specialist knowledge.
- Examples: ML model engineering, video transcoding, real-time bidding, payment-processor integration.
- Reduces cognitive load on stream-aligned teams who would otherwise have to master deeply specialized knowledge.
- Smaller in number than stream-aligned teams; only created when complexity warrants.

4. Platform Team (self-service products)
- Provides internal services that stream-aligned teams consume "as a platform" — i.e., self-service.
- Examples: internal Kubernetes platform, observability platform, CI/CD platform, data platform.
- Treated as a PRODUCT TEAM with internal customers; has roadmap, SLAs to internal users, etc.
- The "Thinnest Viable Platform" principle — only build platform capabilities when they\'re a clear net win for the org.

THE THREE INTERACTION MODES:

1. Collaboration — two teams work closely for a defined period (typically weeks-months) to solve a hard problem. High cognitive load; expensive; time-boxed.

2. X-as-a-Service — one team provides a self-service capability the other consumes. Low cognitive load; standard pattern for Platform teams.

3. Facilitating — one team (usually Enabling) helps another build a capability. Time-boxed knowledge transfer; bridges Collaboration → X-as-a-Service.

CONWAY\'S LAW (Melvin Conway, 1968):
"Organizations which design systems are constrained to produce designs which are copies of the communication structures of these organizations."

Implication: your software architecture WILL mirror your organizational structure. If you want microservices but have monolithic teams, you\'ll get a distributed monolith. The "Inverse Conway Maneuver" — deliberately restructure teams to enable the desired architecture — is the practical move.

COGNITIVE LOAD (Eduardo Salas, 2017+):
- Intrinsic load: complexity of the problem itself.
- Extraneous load: complexity from poor tooling, processes, environments.
- Germane load: building expertise.

Team Topologies argues teams must operate within their cognitive bandwidth. A stream-aligned team that owns 12 microservices, the database, the CI/CD pipeline, AND the K8s cluster has too much extraneous load. Platform teams ABSORB extraneous load.

WHY THIS MATTERS IN INTERVIEWS:

Senior DevOps engineers in 2026 are expected to:
- Diagnose org problems using the Team Topologies vocabulary
- Recognize when a Platform team should be created (or shouldn\'t)
- Apply the Inverse Conway Maneuver
- Spot anti-patterns: "shadow IT" (covert platform teams), "pet platform" (platform team building toys nobody uses), "DevOps team" (re-creates the dev/ops silo)

The book is short and dense. Read it.`,
